<Post title="Beacons and blobs" part={1} date="2026-09-17" series="rshunterbtt">
	<p class="tagline">
		Before rewriting anything, one question had to be answered: does the tap timer broadcast
		its state for anyone listening, or does it only speak when you connect and ask? The
		answer decided the entire architecture — and getting it meant going through a vendored
		Cordova app one decompiled class at a time.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the question</h2>
	<p>
		The timers are Hunter BTT-200s — battery-powered Bluetooth tap timers, each with two
		solenoid zones, screwed onto a garden tap. Three of them cover six zones. Out of the
		box they are programmed by a phone app that has to be within about ten metres of the
		timer, and the app's store rating sits around three stars with a comments section best
		described as "candid". The plan was never to use the app. A small bridge already talked
		to the timers over BLE and surfaced them to the home automation layer, so the phone
		could stay in a pocket.
	</p>
	<p>
		That bridge had one fatal problem: querying a zone's state could take long enough that
		the voice assistant would give up and declare the device offline. A zone that reports
		"offline" half the time is not a zone you can automate. Before rewriting anything, I
		needed to understand where that latency came from — and the first question decided the
		entire architecture: does the timer broadcast its state for anyone listening, or does it
		only speak when you connect and ask?
	</p>
	<p>
		The existing bridge's design was shaped by the assumption that state had to be fetched
		actively: to read zone state it opened a connection, read a characteristic, and let the
		connection fall over. If the state were available passively — in the BLE advertisements
		the timers already broadcast for discovery — the whole problem would collapse into
		"listen and decode".
	</p>
	<p>
		So the first thing I did was settle it: <strong>beacons or GATT?</strong> The vendor
		Android app was the only documentation that existed, so I pulled the APK apart.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>a Cordova app with a tidy little BLE library</h2>
	<p>
		The app turns out to be a Cordova app — the UI is a web bundle, and all the interesting
		behaviour lives in a Java plugin package, <code>com.huiyuan.ble</code>. That is unusually
		pleasant to read: the AIS ("application interface specification") protocol classes are
		one class per characteristic, each with a <code>toBytes</code>/<code>fromBytes</code> pair. It is the closest thing to a protocol specification this hardware has.
	</p>
	<p>
		The scan path is where the beacon question gets answered. Both the modern scanner and
		the legacy one funnel every advertisement into the same three fields — device <em>name</em>, <em>RSSI</em>, and advertised <em>service UUIDs</em>:
	</p>
	<pre><code>l lVarA = l.a(scanResult.getScanRecord().getBytes());
this.f783a.a(device, lVarA.f, scanResult.getRssi(), lVarA.f651b);</code></pre>
	<p>
		The scan-record parser can decode manufacturer data and service data — but the wrapper
		never looks at those fields. It only walks the service-UUID list, and uses it for one
		thing: deciding which generation of device this is. Scanning is discovery-only. There is
		no passive status path to exploit.
	</p>
	<p>
		<strong>Answer: GATT only.</strong> Reliability would have to come from connection
		maintenance, not from eavesdropping. That single fact made the rest of the project a
		connection-reliability problem, not a protocol-decoding one.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>two generations, one UUID trick</h2>
	<p>
		The devices come in two flavours, distinguished purely by the service UUID they
		advertise:
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>generation</th><th>service UUID</th><th>framing</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>first</td>
				<td><code>0000fcc0-…</code></td>
				<td>in-payload <code>[id][len][payload]</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>second</td>
				<td><code>0000ff80-…</code></td>
				<td>no header, no length, no checksum</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		All the deployed timers are second-generation. In that scheme the characteristic <em>is</em> the command: the low byte of the UUID is a protocol ID. The app's protocol
		enum value maps straight onto it — protocol <code>65410</code> is <code>0xff82</code>, and so on. There is no checksum anywhere in the protocol; searching
		the decompiled sources for XOR/CRC comes up empty.
	</p>
	<p>The second-generation profile, as decoded from the app:</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>characteristic</th><th>meaning</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td><code>ff81</code></td><td>4-byte device password (auth at connect)</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>ff82</code></td><td><strong>zone status</strong> — 14 bytes, notification-enabled</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>ff83</code></td><td>overall status — enabled, suspend, run-all timer, zone config</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>ff84</code></td><td>clock</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>ff85</code>–<code>ff88</code></td><td>program schedules</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>ff8a</code> / <code>ff8f</code></td><td>active run times (notification-enabled)</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>ff90</code> / <code>ff91</code></td><td>zone names</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>ff9e</code> / <code>ffa2</code> / <code>ffa3</code></td><td>irrigation history records</td></tr>
			<tr><td><code>2a19</code></td><td>battery level (standard GATT, read)</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		Notifications are enabled during service discovery by writing the standard CCCD <code>00002902</code>. That detail matters enormously later: it means the device can <em>push</em> state changes instead of waiting to be asked.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>the 14-byte status payload</h2>
	<p>
		Everything hinges on <code>ff82</code>. The app parses it into fourteen named fields, and
		the layout is the difference between a bridge that works and one that quietly lies:
	</p>
	<pre><code>byte  0  enabled                  byte  8  zone2EnableManual      *
byte  1  suspendWatering          byte  9  zone2EnableExtManual
byte  2  zone1Enabled             byte 10  zone1State             <-- actual valve state
byte  3  zone1Mode                byte 11  zone2State             <-- actual valve state
byte  4  zone1EnableManual     *  byte 12  zone1Conflict
byte  5  zone1EnableExtManual     byte 13  zone2Conflict
byte  6  zone2Enabled
byte  7  zone2Mode</code></pre>
	<p>
		The bridge I was replacing read zone state from bytes <strong>4 and 8</strong>. Those are
		the <em>manual-enable flags</em> — the "I started this run by hand" markers — not the
		valve state. The bridge happened to look correct for the one case it created itself (it
		always starts zones manually), but it would report the wrong state for anything the
		device started by itself: a scheduled run, or a physical button press.
	</p>
	<p>
		That is a genuinely nasty class of bug, because it is invisible in exactly the situation
		you built it for. It only shows up when the hardware does something you didn't ask it to.
	</p>

	<h2><span class="anchor-hash">#</span>what else the app was doing that the bridge wasn't</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>
			<strong>Writing a password.</strong> The app writes a 4-byte password to <code>ff81</code> on every connect. The bridge never did.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Subscribing to notifications.</strong> The app subscribes to <code>ff82</code>
			(and friends) and reacts to pushes. The bridge only ever polled.
		</li>
		<li>
			<strong>Holding a connection.</strong> The bridge's code had no <code>disconnect</code> call and held its peripheral in a mutex — but every single operation still ran <code>is_connected()</code> and, finding false, did a full ten-second scan →
			match-by-address → connect → discover cycle first. In production logs, that meant a
			complete reconnect before nearly every command. One unit logged thousands of
			"not connected, reconnecting" cycles.
		</li>
	</ul>
	<p>
		So the picture was clear to me before I wrote a line of code: the protocol was
		understood, the status bug was identified, and the reliability problem was
		architectural. The next question was whether the hardware would actually cooperate —
		which is a question you can only answer by going and poking it.
	</p>

	<p>
		Next: taking a purpose-built probe to the live devices, and discovering that the
		Raspberry Pi's own Bluetooth controller was going to be a recurring character.
	</p>
</Post>
